ea, Fasilides founding Gondar in the 1630s (Gondarine period), and the Zemene Mesafint decentralization after the deaths of Iyasu II (1755) and Iyoas I (1769) [same source].\n\nThe Britannica ABYSSINIA fragment is much narrower: an early-18th-century court episode. It concerns Tecla Haimanout, the killing of M. de Roule, threatened hostilities against the king of Sennaar, the rival Amda Sion backed by friends of \"his father Yasous,\" Tecla Haimanout's death, and the reign of his uncle Tiffilis/Theophilus, including executions and a battle with Tigi [Britannica, \"ABYSSINIA\", entry_id `page-2570-364adfefc0b1:e1`, page_id `britannica:309f765b1ecc83e0a7f7762e44b4a927a7555443:train:2570`].\n\n**Overlap.** Shared vocabulary includes death, emperor, gondar, king, throne, capital, clergy, abyssinia, army, people [Britannica vocabulary block, entry_id `page-2570-364adfefc0b1:e1`]. \"Gondar\" and \"emperor\" appearing in both is consistent with the Gondarine-period material. Britannica entry-only words — tecla, theophilus, abyssinian, assassinated, murderers, roule, perished, galla — are the episode-specific names and vocabulary not prominent in the matched Wikipedia article [same vocabulary block]. Entry coverage 0.3792 vs baseline 0.0961 (lift ≈ 3.95) shows the Britannica text is topically aligned with the Ethiopian Empire article [same vocabulary block].\n\n## Fact-claim check (Britannica claims vs saved Wikipedia passages)\n\n`challenge_compare` checked four Britannica claims against the saved Wikipedia passages. All four returned verdict **not_found**: the Britannica quotation is present in the OCR (`britannica_quote_found: true`), but the claim is not found in the saved Wikipedia passages (`wikipedia_quote_found: false`). Per the evidence rules I treat these as Britannica-only claims, not confirmed by the saved Wikipedia evidence:\n\n| Claim | Britannica quote [entry_id `page-2570-364adfefc0b1:e1`, page_id `britannica:309f765b1ecc83e0a7f7762e44b4a927a7555443:train:25